In this lesson, we'll look at simplifying expressions that involve brackets with fraction coefficients, like something times something plus something. The first approach is the distribute and collect method. You multiply each term in the first bracket by each term in the second, then group up all your x terms together and all your plain number terms together. After that, you just simplify the fractions by doing the addition or subtraction, like adding one half and one quarter to get three quarters. The second approach is sometimes quicker. Before you even start multiplying, look for a factor that appears in every single term. If you can spot one, pull it out front and you might avoid a lot of unnecessary work. Both roads end up at the same simplified expression, so pick whichever one feels easier for the numbers in front of you. Now let's see it in action.